  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nBroad upper level trough with a trailing cold front will move\nacross the state this morning. With this will come chances for\nisolated to perhaps scattered showers, although activity\nupstream looks fairly unorganized and may continue to be as this\nwave moves through. Some steep mid level lapse rates and low \ninstability could provide for a few thunderstorms as well this \nmorning, with the threat for severe weather very low. Behind\nthis front will be some breezy northwest winds later today into\nthis evening. Lowering RH values could bring some increased fire\nweather concerns today, mainly in the far southwest where\nafternoon humidity values approach 30 percent. Highs today will\nbe tempered slightly by this front and generally in the 60s,\nwith lower 70s southeast. Winds then diminish tonight, with\nskies also expected to clear. Dry conditions should limit fog\npotential tonight. Clearing skies may also bring some cooler\nlows tonight in the 30s to lower 40s. Perhaps some isolated near\nfreezing temperatures are possible tonight. NBM 10th percentile\nforecast still keeps most areas at or above freezing, thus \nconfidence in a freeze highlight is low at this time. Surface \nhigh will then be found across the state for Sunday, bringing \nmainly sunny skies, lighter winds, and near normal highs in the \n60s to lower 70s. Mainly clear skies look to remain for Sunday \nnight, although a switch to southerly flow could bring more mild\novernight lows in the mid 30s to mid 40s. \n\nZonal flow becoming a northwest flow aloft still looks to settle\nin for early to mid next week. The result will be warming\ntemperatures with mainly dry conditions. Forecast highs each day\nare currently in the upper 60s to upper 70s, with perhaps some\nlower 80s Monday. These warmer temperatures and drying\nconditions will bring lower afternoon humidity values, with 20s\npossible in the southwest. A mainly dry surface low passing\nthrough Monday and Tuesday could bring some breezy southerly\nwinds Monday, becoming a breezy northwest wind on Tuesday. The\nresult could be some increased fire weather concerns each day,\nto perhaps near critical fire weather conditions especially in\nthe southwest. Today's forecast did lower the winds somewhat on\nTuesday, which lowered this fire weather threat slightly. Breezy\nsoutherly winds on Monday are also forecast to be more central\nand east away from the lower RH in the west. Thus will continue\nto monitor these fire weather concerns yet hold off on near \ncritical fire weather messaging at this time. Temperature\nspreads remain slightly increased for later next week, although\nthe overall trend is near to perhaps slightly above normal.\nClusters indicate northwest flow aloft with ridging to our west\nas the favored weather pattern. This favors a mainly dry\nforecast and the mentioned above normal temperatures to end next work\nweek.",
